      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is not clear to me that we would want to do this. For Android which does not use a winsys integration, it is not even clear to me that we should even be building/including the mesa stuff at all.\n\nIMO, I think we should have\n\n1. Android\u0027s Vulkan entry points should be the \"existing Gfxstream\"/\"Gfxstream ResourceTracker/Encoder\" layer directly which use the existing `goldfish_\u003cVK_TYPE\u003e` (IMO should be renamed to `gfxstream_\u003cVK_TYPE\u003e`). \n\n2. \"Gfxstream Linux\" Vulkan entry points are the mesa functions which additionally wrap all of the `goldfish_\u003cVK_TYPE\u003e` handles with `gfxstream_mesa_\u003cVK_TYPE\u003e` handles and provide the additional functionality on top.\n\n3. We should consolidate the existing logging in the \"existing Gfxstream\" layer into a `gfxstream_log` setup similar to `stream_renderer_debug` which defaults to a per-platform logger (ALOGE for Android, stdout/stderr for Linux, etc). The \"Gfxstream Linux\" build could register a `gfxstream_mesa_log()` on first use.\n\nHonestly, given #2, I think the \"Gfxstream mesa winsys integration\" should really be implemented as a proper Vulkan Layer instead of mixing parts of Mesa into Gfxstream. IIUC, you could replace all of the \n\n```\nauto resources \u003d gfxstream::vk::ResourceTracker::get();\nresource-\u003eon_vkFunctionCall()\n```\n\nin the `hardware/google/Gfxstream/guest/Vulkan/gfxstream_vk*` files with calls into any other Vulkan implementation and nothing would change. This suggests that it should not even be part of Gfxstream.",
